1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,679
First we enter a short request: fix
validation.

2
00:00:03,859 --> 00:00:08,543
It says little about the problem, the
affected files, or the result we need.

3
00:00:08,723 --> 00:00:12,915
A model might suggest several changes that
all sound reasonable.

4
00:00:13,095 --> 00:00:18,430
Before we accept any proposal, we will
turn that request into a brief that makes

5
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:22,299
the defect, scope, and acceptance criteria
easy to review.

6
00:00:23,757 --> 00:00:25,926
Start with the behavior in the issue.

7
00:00:26,106 --> 00:00:29,146
An order with zero items currently passes.

8
00:00:29,326 --> 00:00:33,006
The documented rule says the quantity must
be positive.

9
00:00:33,186 --> 00:00:38,366
We give the assistant the source function
and its existing tests, using the approved

10
00:00:38,366 --> 00:00:39,415
training context.

11
00:00:39,595 --> 00:00:43,255
This lets it connect the actual comparison
to the requirement.

12
00:00:43,435 --> 00:00:47,617
Avoid unexplained assumptions about the
rest of the repository.

13
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:51,028
Next, state the boundaries.

14
00:00:51,208 --> 00:00:53,133
Keep the validator's signature.

15
00:00:53,313 --> 00:00:56,249
Limit the work to the validator and its
test file.

16
00:00:56,429 --> 00:00:58,521
The inputs one and five should pass.

17
00:00:58,701 --> 00:01:01,041
Zero and negative one should fail.

18
00:01:01,221 --> 00:01:05,499
These examples give a reviewer a concrete
way to judge the result.

19
00:01:05,679 --> 00:01:10,533
They also help the assistant notice if its
suggested fix misses a boundary.

20
00:01:12,007 --> 00:01:14,163
Now specify what you want back.

21
00:01:14,343 --> 00:01:19,273
Ask for the cause, a list of proposed
files, and the regression test before

22
00:01:19,273 --> 00:01:20,785
seeking implementation.

23
00:01:20,965 --> 00:01:25,678
If relevant information is missing, tell
the assistant to say what it needs.

24
00:01:25,858 --> 00:01:30,818
Once you approve the plan, ask it to
report the exact test command and outcome.

25
00:01:30,998 --> 00:01:33,399
The format serves your review process.

26
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:39,682
Suppose the assistant suggests renaming
the function and refactoring callers.

27
00:01:39,862 --> 00:01:44,725
We can point to the unchanged signature
requirement and request a revised plan.

28
00:01:44,905 --> 00:01:48,702
Useful follow ups give precise feedback
about the mismatch.

29
00:01:48,882 --> 00:01:53,597
After the second response, check that the
proposed test would fail against the

30
00:01:53,597 --> 00:01:55,048
original implementation.

31
00:01:55,228 --> 00:01:59,389
Keep the brief available so the next turn
stays tied to the same scope.

32
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:04,115
Write one line that you can verify after
the change.

33
00:02:04,295 --> 00:02:09,094
The new zero test should fail on the
starting code and pass on the proposed

34
00:02:09,094 --> 00:02:09,350
fix.

35
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:11,718
The existing tests should still pass.

36
00:02:11,898 --> 00:02:15,333
This gives the assistant and the reviewer
the same target.

37
00:02:15,513 --> 00:02:20,340
Keep the exact test command and the
observed outcome separate from a proposed

38
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:20,654
plan.

39
00:02:22,132 --> 00:02:24,152
Choose another synthetic ticket.

40
00:02:24,332 --> 00:02:29,298
Write the goal, the relevant sources, the
scope, the acceptance criteria, and the

41
00:02:29,298 --> 00:02:30,708
evidence you want back.

42
00:02:30,888 --> 00:02:35,503
Run your prompt in the approved tool and
compare the response with those five

43
00:02:35,503 --> 00:02:35,922
pieces.

44
00:02:36,102 --> 00:02:41,128
If the response is weak, change one part
of the brief and try again so you can see

45
00:02:41,128 --> 00:02:42,354
which change helped.
